    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="hm-what">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="split split--top">
          <div>
            <p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Overview</p>
            <h2 class="title" id="hm-what" data-fx="wipe">What is <span class="grad-text">hematology?</span></h2>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Dr. Neema Bhat, a US-trained hematologist in Bangalore with 10+ years of experience, offers complete hematology treatment in Bangalore for adults and children, from simple anemia to bone marrow transplant.</p>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Blood disorders are very common in India. About 48 out of every 100 women and two out of three young children in Karnataka have anemia (NFHS-5). In 2022, about 55,600 people in India were diagnosed with leukemia, 55,300 with lymphoma and 19,400 with multiple myeloma (National Cancer Registry Programme, ICMR). An abnormal blood report is often the first clue, and the right hematology treatment in Bangalore starts with finding the exact cause.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg>Your blood, in simple words</h3><p class="box__sub">Your blood has three main types of cells, all made in the bone marrow:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Red blood cells</b> carry oxygen to every part of your body.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>White blood cells</b> fight infections.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Platelets</b> help your blood clot and stop bleeding.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Your blood also contains clotting proteins that work with platelets to seal a cut. When any of these are too low, too high or not working properly, it is called a blood disorder.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="cols2 mt">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-doc"/></svg>What does a hematologist do?</h3><p class="box__sub">A hematologist is a doctor who specialises in blood disorders. A hematologist:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Finds out why your blood counts or blood tests are abnormal</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Treats anemia, bleeding and clotting disorders, and low or high blood counts</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Treats blood cancers such as leukemia, lymphoma and myeloma (a hemato-oncologist)</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Performs bone marrow tests and bone marrow (stem cell) transplants</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Works with other doctors, such as gynaecologists, surgeons and kidney specialists, when a blood problem affects their care</span></li></ul></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-child"/></svg>Pediatric hematology</h3><p class="box__sub">A pediatric hematologist treats blood disorders in babies, children and teenagers. Dr. Neema Bhat is trained in both adult and pediatric hematology.</p><p class="box__sub">Children may look pale, bruise easily, bleed for a long time after a fall or vaccine, get infections often, or have swollen glands or a swollen tummy. Children with these signs should see a pediatric hematologist.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="hm-signs">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Signs</p><h2 class="title" id="hm-signs" data-fx="wipe">Signs you should see a <span class="grad-text">hematologist</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Many blood disorders are first found on a routine blood test. See a hematologist if you have:</p>
        </div><ul class="sym sym--3"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><b>Low hemoglobin</b><span>Anemia that keeps coming back or does not improve with iron tablets</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-activity"/></svg><b>Constant tiredness</b><span>Weakness, breathlessness or pale skin</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg><b>Easy bruising</b><span>Small red or purple spots on the skin (petechiae)</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><b>Frequent bleeding</b><span>Nosebleeds, bleeding gums, or heavy or long periods</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><b>Bleeding that won’t stop</b><span>After a cut, tooth extraction or surgery</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><b>Leg swelling or pain</b><span>Swelling, pain or redness in one leg, possibly a clot (DVT)</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg><b>Repeated clots</b><span>Blood clots at a young age, or repeated clots or miscarriages</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg><b>Abnormal counts</b><span>Low or high platelet, white cell or red cell counts on your report</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-thermo"/></svg><b>Frequent infections</b><span>Infections that are frequent or unusual</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg><b>Swollen lymph nodes</b><span>Glands in the neck, armpit or groin</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-scale"/></svg><b>Unexplained symptoms</b><span>Fever, night sweats or weight loss without a clear reason</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-bone"/></svg><b>Bone pain</b><span>Especially in the back</span></li></ul><p class="alert" data-fx="fade"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><span><b>See a doctor urgently</b> if you have heavy bleeding that will not stop, sudden chest pain or breathlessness, sudden weakness on one side of the body, blood in vomit or black stools, high fever with very low white cell counts, or fainting.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="hm-cause">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="cols2">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="hm-cause"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg>What causes blood disorders?</h3><p class="box__sub">Blood disorders have many causes. The common ones are:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Nutritional deficiency:</b> low iron, vitamin B12 or folate.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Blood loss:</b> heavy periods, piles, ulcers or slow bleeding in the gut.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Inherited (genetic) conditions:</b> thalassemia, sickle cell disease, hemophilia and some clotting disorders.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Immune system problems:</b> the body attacks its own blood cells, as in ITP or autoimmune hemolytic anemia.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Long-term illnesses:</b> kidney disease, liver disease, thyroid problems and autoimmune diseases.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Infections:</b> such as dengue, malaria, viral infections and worm infections.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Medicines:</b> some painkillers, antibiotics, blood thinners and chemotherapy.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Bone marrow problems:</b> aplastic anemia, myelodysplastic syndromes and blood cancers.</span></li></ul></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg>Who is at risk?</h3><p class="box__sub">The risk is higher for:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Women with heavy periods, and pregnant or breastfeeding women</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Babies, children and teenagers, who grow fast</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Vegetarians and vegans, who may get too little vitamin B12</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Adults over 60</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>People with a family history of blood disorders</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Couples where both partners are thalassemia carriers</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>People with long-term illnesses or who take certain medicines for a long time</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>People who smoke or have had radiation or chemotherapy</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>People who are overweight, inactive, or sit for long hours (higher clot risk)</span></li></ul></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="hm-untr">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Complications</p><h2 class="title" id="hm-untr" data-fx="wipe">What happens if blood disorders are <span class="grad-text">left untreated?</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Some blood problems seem mild at first but can become serious over time:</p>
        </div><ul class="conds"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg>Heart strain</b><p>Long-term anemia makes the heart work harder and can lead to heart failure.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg>Dangerous bleeding</b><p>Low platelets or clotting disorders can cause bleeding into joints, the gut or the brain.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-lungs"/></svg>Blood clots</b><p>An untreated clot in the leg can travel to the lungs (pulmonary embolism) and be life-threatening.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-thermo"/></svg>Serious infections</b><p>Low white cell counts make infections more likely and harder to treat.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg>Organ damage</b><p>Iron overload from repeated transfusions can harm the heart and liver.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-child"/></svg>Pregnancy problems</b><p>Anemia and clotting disorders raise the risk of miscarriage, preterm birth and heavy bleeding.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-micro"/></svg>Late cancer diagnosis</b><p>An abnormal blood count can be the first sign of leukemia, lymphoma or myeloma. Early diagnosis improves treatment results.</p></li></ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="hm-dis">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head head--center">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Conditions</p><h2 class="title" id="hm-dis" data-fx="wipe">Blood disorders <span class="grad-text">we treat</span></h2></div>
          
        </div><div class="dtable-wrap" data-fx="fade"><table class="dtable"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Conditions</th><th>What it means in simple words</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label="Group"><b>Red blood cell disorders</b></td><td data-label="Conditions"><a href="/anemia-treatment">Iron deficiency anemia</a>, vitamin B12 and folate deficiency, anemia of chronic disease, hemolytic anemia, polycythemia</td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Too few red cells, red cells that break down early, or too many red cells.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Group"><b>Inherited blood disorders</b></td><td data-label="Conditions"><a href="/thalassemia-treatment">Thalassemia</a>, sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis, G6PD deficiency</td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Conditions passed down in families that affect how red cells are made or how long they last.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Group"><b>Platelet disorders</b></td><td data-label="Conditions">ITP (immune thrombocytopenia), low platelets in dengue or pregnancy, high platelets (thrombocytosis)</td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Too few or too many platelets, causing bleeding or clotting.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Group"><b>Bleeding disorders</b></td><td data-label="Conditions">Hemophilia A and B, von Willebrand disease, other clotting factor deficiencies</td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">The blood does not clot properly, so bleeding lasts longer.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Group"><b>Clotting (thrombosis) disorders</b></td><td data-label="Conditions">Deep vein thrombosis (DVT), pulmonary embolism, thrombophilia, antiphospholipid syndrome</td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">The blood clots too easily, blocking veins or arteries.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Group"><b>White blood cell disorders</b></td><td data-label="Conditions">Low white cell count (neutropenia), high white cell count</td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Too few white cells to fight infection, or too many for an unclear reason.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Group"><b>Bone marrow failure</b></td><td data-label="Conditions">Aplastic anemia, pure red cell aplasia</td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">The bone marrow stops making enough blood cells.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Group"><b>Blood cancers</b></td><td data-label="Conditions">Acute and chronic <a href="/leukemia-treatment">leukemia</a>, Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin <a href="/lymphoma-treatment">lymphoma</a>, <a href="/multiple-myeloma-treatment">multiple myeloma</a>, myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS), myeloproliferative neoplasms (MPN)</td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Cancers that start in the bone marrow, blood or lymph nodes.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Group"><b>Immune and rare disorders</b></td><td data-label="Conditions">HLH (hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis), primary immunodeficiency disorders, thrombotic microangiopathy</td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Rare conditions where the immune system or small blood vessels damage blood cells.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Group"><b>Children’s blood disorders</b></td><td data-label="Conditions">Anemia, thalassemia, ITP, hemophilia and childhood leukemia</td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Specialised care for babies, children and teenagers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="hm-dx">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Diagnosis</p><h2 class="title" id="hm-dx" data-fx="wipe">How are blood disorders <span class="grad-text">diagnosed?</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">The right test depends on your symptoms and blood report. Dr. Neema Bhat may advise some of these tests:</p>
        </div><ol class="olist"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">01</span><div><b>Complete Blood Count (CBC)</b><p>Checks hemoglobin, white cells and platelets.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">02</span><div><b>Peripheral blood smear</b><p>Looks at the shape and size of blood cells under a microscope.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">03</span><div><b>Reticulocyte count</b><p>Shows whether the bone marrow is making new red cells.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">04</span><div><b>Iron studies, vitamin B12 and folate</b><p>Find nutritional causes of anemia.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">05</span><div><b>Hemoglobin electrophoresis / HPLC</b><p>Checks for thalassemia and sickle cell disease.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">06</span><div><b>Coagulation tests</b><p>PT/INR, APTT, fibrinogen and D-dimer check how well your blood clots.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">07</span><div><b>Clotting factor assays &amp; von Willebrand tests</b><p>Diagnose hemophilia and other bleeding disorders.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">08</span><div><b>Thrombophilia screening</b><p>Looks for inherited or acquired causes of repeated blood clots.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">09</span><div><b>Flow cytometry (immunophenotyping)</b><p>Identifies abnormal cells in leukemia and lymphoma.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">10</span><div><b>Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy</b><p>Examines the bone marrow directly.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">11</span><div><b>Cytogenetics, FISH &amp; molecular tests</b><p>Find genetic changes that guide treatment of blood cancers.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">12</span><div><b>Lymph node biopsy</b><p>Diagnoses lymphoma.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">13</span><div><b>Imaging</b><p>Ultrasound, Doppler, CT or PET-CT find clots, enlarged lymph nodes or spleen, and check the spread of blood cancers.</p></div></li></ol>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="hm-tx">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Treatment</p><h2 class="title" id="hm-tx" data-fx="wipe">Hematology treatment <span class="grad-text">options</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Treatment depends on the type of blood disorder, its cause, your age and your overall health. Some conditions need only a short course of treatment, while others need long-term care.</p>
        </div><div class="cards3 cards3--5"><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-apple"/></svg><h3>Nutritional supplements</h3><p>Iron, vitamin B12 and folic acid as tablets, syrups or injections.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-syringe"/></svg><h3>IV iron infusion</h3><p>A faster way to replace iron when tablets do not work or cannot be taken.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg><h3>Treating the root cause</h3><p>Such as heavy periods, gut bleeding, infections, thyroid or kidney problems, with the right specialist.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-syringe"/></svg><h3>Blood transfusion</h3><p>Red cells, platelets or plasma, for severe anemia, bleeding or low counts.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg><h3>Iron chelation therapy</h3><p>Removes extra iron in patients who need regular transfusions, such as in thalassemia.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><h3>Clotting factor replacement</h3><p>Including preventive (prophylaxis) therapy for hemophilia and other bleeding disorders.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-lungs"/></svg><h3>Anticoagulation therapy</h3><p>Blood-thinning treatment that treats and prevents blood clots, with regular monitoring.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><h3>Immune-suppressing therapy</h3><p>For ITP, autoimmune hemolytic anemia and aplastic anemia.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-pill"/></svg><h3>Count-boosting medicines</h3><p>To raise platelets, white cells or red cells when needed.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-syringe"/></svg><h3>Chemotherapy</h3><p>Anti-cancer medicines for leukemia, lymphoma and myeloma.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-pill"/></svg><h3>Targeted therapy</h3><p>Medicines that attack specific changes in cancer cells, often as tablets.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-award"/></svg><h3>Immunotherapy</h3><p>Monoclonal antibodies and other treatments that help the immune system fight blood cancers.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg><h3>CAR-T cell therapy</h3><p>For selected relapsed blood cancers, where suitable and available.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-marrow"/></svg><h3>Autologous stem cell transplant</h3><p>Your own stem cells are returned after high-dose treatment, mainly for myeloma and lymphoma.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-marrow"/></svg><h3>Allogeneic (donor) stem cell transplant</h3><p>Healthy stem cells from a matched or half-matched donor. It can cure thalassemia, sickle cell disease, aplastic anemia and some leukemias. <a href="/bone-marrow-transplant">Learn more</a></p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><h3>Therapeutic phlebotomy</h3><p>Removing blood to lower high red cell counts or iron levels.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg><h3>Plasmapheresis</h3><p>Plasma exchange removes harmful antibodies or proteins from the blood.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><h3>Supportive care</h3><p>Infection prevention, vaccines, pain relief and nutrition support.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-chat"/></svg><h3>Counselling &amp; palliative care</h3><p>Emotional support and symptom control for patients and families.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-clock"/></svg><h3>Watch and wait</h3><p>Active monitoring for mild or slow-growing conditions that do not need treatment yet.</p></div></div><p class="alert alert--warn" data-fx="fade"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><span><b>Do not take iron tablets, blood thinners or steroids for a long time without a doctor’s advice.</b> Wrong or delayed treatment can hide a serious cause.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="hm-live">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="cols2">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="hm-live"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-sun"/></svg>Living well and preventing blood disorders</h3><p class="box__sub">Not every blood disorder can be prevented, but these steps help:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Eat iron-rich foods:</b> ragi, green leafy vegetables (palak, methi, drumstick leaves), dals, rajma, chana, jaggery, dates, eggs, chicken, fish and meat.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Add vitamin C to meals</b> (lemon, amla, orange, guava) to help your body absorb iron, and avoid tea or coffee within one hour of meals.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Get enough vitamin B12:</b> milk, curd, paneer, eggs and fish. Vegetarians may need supplements.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Prevent blood clots:</b> stay active, drink enough water, and walk or stretch during long journeys.</span></li></ul></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg>Protect yourself</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Get a thalassemia carrier test (HPLC)</b> before marriage or pregnancy. If both partners are carriers, each child has a 1 in 4 chance of thalassemia major.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Protect yourself from infections:</b> use mosquito protection against dengue and malaria, and take deworming tablets as advised.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Avoid self-medication:</b> some painkillers and other medicines affect platelets and blood counts.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>If you have a bleeding disorder:</b> tell your dentist and surgeon before any procedure, and carry a medical card.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Keep every follow-up appointment and repeat blood tests as advised.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">India’s Anemia Mukt Bharat programme gives free iron and folic acid tablets to children, teenagers, and pregnant and breastfeeding women through government health centres.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

