              <a class="btn btn--line btn--sm" href="tel:+917899756677"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-phone"/></svg>Call</a>
            </div>
          </div>
          
        </div>
        <div class="faq2__list"><details class="qa" open><summary><span class="qa__n">01</span><span class="qa__q">What does a hematologist treat?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">A hematologist treats diseases of the blood, bone marrow and lymph nodes. This includes anemia, bleeding and clotting disorders, low or high blood counts, thalassemia, sickle cell disease and blood cancers like leukemia, lymphoma and myeloma.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">02</span><span class="qa__q">When should I see a hematologist?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">See a hematologist if your blood report shows abnormal counts, if anemia does not improve with treatment, or if you have easy bruising, unusual bleeding, repeated blood clots, swollen glands or a family history of a blood disorder.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">03</span><span class="qa__q">Is a hematologist the same as an oncologist?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Not exactly. A hematologist treats all blood disorders, both non-cancerous and cancerous. A hemato-oncologist specialises in blood cancers. An oncologist treats cancers of other organs. Dr. Neema Bhat treats both blood disorders and blood cancers.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">04</span><span class="qa__q">Does being referred to a hematologist mean I have cancer?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">No. Most people seen by a hematologist have non-cancerous problems like anemia, low platelets or clotting issues. A referral simply means a blood specialist will find the cause faster.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">05</span><span class="qa__q">What happens at the first hematology appointment?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">The doctor will ask about your symptoms, medical and family history, and medicines. She will examine you and review your reports. You may need more blood tests, and rarely a bone marrow test.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">06</span><span class="qa__q">What should I bring to my first visit?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Bring all recent blood reports, scans, discharge summaries and a list of your current medicines. Older reports help show how your counts have changed over time.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">07</span><span class="qa__q">Are blood disorders curable?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Many are. Nutritional anemias are usually cured once the cause is fixed. Some conditions like hemophilia need lifelong care, while thalassemia, sickle cell disease, aplastic anemia and some blood cancers can be cured with a bone marrow transplant.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">08</span><span class="qa__q">Is a bone marrow test painful?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">The area is numbed with local anaesthesia, so most people feel only pressure and brief discomfort. The test usually takes 15 to 30 minutes, and you can go home the same day.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">09</span><span class="qa__q">Can children see a hematologist?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Yes. Dr. Neema Bhat is a US board-certified pediatrician and pediatric hematologist. She treats babies, children and teenagers with anemia, thalassemia, ITP, hemophilia and childhood leukemia.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">10</span><span class="qa__q">How much does hematology treatment in Bangalore cost?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">The cost depends on the condition. Blood tests and tablets are usually affordable, while transfusions, chemotherapy and bone marrow transplant cost more. The clinic can share an estimate after your first consultation.</p></details></div>
